# Provider binaries and module caches — re-downloaded by `terraform init`.
.terraform/

# State. This configuration keeps state in S3 (see each env's backend block);
# these patterns exist so a local `terraform apply` run without the backend
# cannot leave real infrastructure state in the repository. State files contain
# every attribute of every resource, including generated passwords.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
.terraform.tfstate.lock.info

# Variable files. The inputs are account ids, hosted zone ids and certificate
# ARNs — pass them on the command line or through TF_VAR_*, not a committed file.
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
!example.tfvars

# Plan output is a binary containing resolved variable VALUES, secrets included.
*.tfplan

# NOT ignored, deliberately: .terraform.lock.hcl
#
# The dependency lock file pins provider versions and records their checksums —
# the Terraform equivalent of package-lock.json. Committing it is what makes an
# apply reproducible and stops a provider release silently changing what the
# next plan does. `terraform init` in CI verifies against it.
